=== axis 11 (ops) — hook fail-open / silent-disable probes ===
All runs against a sandbox copy of the repo + a throwaway `harness init`-ed project,
never against the real working tree. Repro commands use literal paths (no shell vars)
so they can be pasted as-is.

--- Probe 1: delete core/dist/cli.js, then fire the PreToolUse hook for a P0-blocked write ---
$ mv core/dist/cli.js core/dist/cli.js.bak
$ CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=<proj> node bin/harness hook pre-tool < payload.json
exit=0
stdout: <empty, 0 bytes>
stderr: "king-wjang-harness: no build in core/dist — run `npm install` (prepare) or `npm run prepare` to build it."
$ mv core/dist/cli.js.bak core/dist/cli.js   # restore

Baseline (dist present) for the SAME payload, for contrast:
stdout: {"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"PreToolUse","permissionDecision":"deny","permissionDecisionReason":"Implementation code cannot be written in the design track (P0) — src/index.ts is blocked ... Finish the design artifacts first."}}

Conclusion: with dist missing, PreToolUse emits no hookSpecificOutput/permissionDecision at
all (not even an explicit "allow" — just nothing) and exits 0. Per hooks.json's contract
(deny is only communicated via that JSON on stdout), an empty stdout + exit 0 is indistinguishable
from "no opinion" and the tool call proceeds unblocked. The only signal is one stderr line that
does not say "protection is currently off" — it reads like a generic install-incomplete notice.
This is bin/harness's own documented "do-no-harm" contract (bin/harness:2-5): hook path is silent
by design when core/dist is missing. All 4 hook events (session-start/pre-tool/post-tool/stop) share
this exact code path (bin/harness:6-12), so this is not pre-tool-specific.

For contrast, an ORDINARY (non-hook) command surfaces this loudly:
$ mv core/dist/cli.js core/dist/cli.js.bak
$ node bin/harness doctor
exit=1
stderr: "king-wjang-harness: no build in core/dist — run `npm install` (prepare) or `npm run prepare` to build it."
$ mv core/dist/cli.js.bak core/dist/cli.js

--- Probe 2: make .harness/ read-only, PreToolUse still functions (read-only judging works) ---
$ chmod -R 555 .harness
$ CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=<proj> node bin/harness hook pre-tool < payload.json
exit=0
stdout: {"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"PreToolUse","permissionDecision":"deny","permissionDecisionReason":"Implementation code cannot be written in the design track (P0) ..."}}
-> the write-blocking judgement itself still works under a read-only .harness/ (it only needs
   to READ config/state, not write). See ax11-doctor-blindspots.log for what silently breaks
   instead (activity-marker tracking / stop-guard).

--- Probe 3: corrupt events.jsonl mid-line while state.json stays valid/parseable ---
$ cp .harness/events.jsonl .harness/events.jsonl.orig
$ { head -c 30 .harness/events.jsonl.orig; echo "not json garbage {{{"; } > .harness/events.jsonl
$ cat .harness/events.jsonl
{"ts":"2026-08-27T14:44:43.380not json garbage {{{

$ node bin/harness doctor
{
  "ok": true,
  "warnings": ["1 line(s) of events.jsonl are corrupt — the replay is incomplete", ...],
  ...
}
-> doctor DOES detect this (as expected — see ax11-doctor-blindspots.log for what it misses).

$ CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=<proj> node bin/harness hook session-start < ss-payload.json
stdout: {"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"SessionStart","additionalContext":"[king-wjang-harness] phase: P0 | active wave: none\nDesign track — writing implementation code ... Next: `harness status` ...  (Silence this with `remote_control: false` in `.harness/config.yaml`.)"}}
-> grep -io "corrupt\|손상\|doctor" on this output: NO MATCH. Zero mention of the corrupted
   journal anywhere in the session-start banner (the one thing that unconditionally fires at
   the top of every session and is meant to orient the user/agent).

$ CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR=<proj> node bin/harness hook pre-tool < payload.json
stdout: {"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"PreToolUse","permissionDecision":"deny","permissionDecisionReason":"Implementation code cannot be written in the design track (P0) ..."}}
-> deny reason also carries NO "[state damaged ...]" tag (compare core/src/hook.ts:645-649's
   `deny()` tag mechanism, which only fires when the local `degraded` variable is non-null).

Root cause (code, core/src/hook.ts:296-317): `degraded` is only ever assigned inside the
`catch` block that fires when `readState(root)` throws or its shape is invalid (i.e. when
state.json ITSELF is broken). If state.json parses fine, the `try` branch succeeds and
`degraded` stays `null` for the rest of this hook call — regardless of how corrupt
events.jsonl is. `degradedNote`/the deny `tag` are only ever populated from that same
`degraded` variable (core/src/hook.ts:360-, :645-649, :2401), so a corrupt journal with an
intact state.json is invisible through every interactive surface (session-start banner,
pre-tool deny reason, stop-guard note) — the ONLY way to learn about it is to proactively
run `harness doctor`, which nothing prompts the user to do.
